// src/v4/extdeps/coordination.dag
//
// Scope: Multi-program coordination — distributed deployment + sync/async/stream/pubsub semantics.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributed_computing +
//         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messaging_pattern (request-reply = sync;
//         fire-and-forget = async; publish-subscribe = pubsub; stream = pipe)
//         + https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inter-process_communication
// Design note (NOT the anchor): coordination is effect-typed not behavior-typed
//   (IN-B decision; no 6th L1 behavior) — see memory feedback_construction_over_ratchets
//   + THESIS:215-221 omni-emission. The anchor above is the external-spec
//   authority per STRUCTURE.md extdeps anchor convention; this line is rationale.
//
// Owns:
//   - Endpoint { address: NetworkAddress, language: LanguageRef, framework: Option<FrameworkRef> }
//   - DeploymentUnit { endpoints: List<Endpoint>, wire_contracts: List<WireContract> }
//   - WireContract { from: Endpoint, to: Endpoint, semantics: CoordinationSemantics }
//   - CoordinationSemantics = Sync | Async(SettleBound) | Stream | PubSub
//     | EventuallyConsistent(ConvergeBound)   (closed enum)
//     The non-immediate-settlement variants carry their bound as a
//     STRUCTURAL field on the carrier (operator-ratified 2026-05-15):
//     "eventually" / "async-settled" is substrate the generated simulator
//     READS to evaluate deterministically (lens/testgen.dag
//     simulator-generation arm) — never a wall-clock harness parameter.
//     A WireContract that cannot express its bound is a substrate STOP,
//     not a silently-unbounded simulation. (SettleBound vs ConvergeBound
//     are distinct semantic quantities — fire-and-forget ack vs replica
//     agreement — kept separate, not prematurely unified. Bound's internal
//     representation is T-4.8 fill detail, gated by the STOP rule.)
//   - These are TYPED CARRIERS over existing 5 L1 behaviors (per IN-B decision);
//     NO 6th behavior introduced — coordination is Bind composition over
//     effect-TYPED operations (effects intrinsic to the type signature, NOT
//     an annotation layer — same stance as unenumerated_effects.dag; consistent
//     with this file's line 8 "effect-typed not behavior-typed")
//   - Effect types per coordination kind (HttpEffect / QueueEffect / StreamEffect / PubSubEffect)
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag, std/nat.dag
//   - extdeps/languages/*.dag (per-endpoint language)
//   - extdeps/frameworks/*.dag (per-endpoint framework, optional)
//
// Status: T-4.8 Lane C modeled 2026-05-16 (quiet-newt-73) — IN-B Bind
//   composition + effect typing; resolver-instance seam intentionally
//   deferred until a named consumer asks for concrete endpoint resolution.
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.coordination


import v4.std.collection { List }
import v4.std.diagnostic { Outcome }
import v4.std.nat { Nat }
import v4.std.node { Node }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (T-4.8 Lane C)
//
// DATA-ONLY COORDINATION SURFACE. This file declares the vocabulary that
// distributed programs and simulator/testgen consumers read: endpoints,
// deployment units, wire contracts, coordination semantics, and the
// effect-typed Bind carrier. It deliberately does not resolve concrete
// endpoint instances, open sockets, spawn runtimes, or lower to any
// transport. Those are consumers of this model, not alternate authorities.
//
// IN-B: BIND COMPOSITION + EFFECT TYPING, NO SIXTH BEHAVIOR. A coordinated
// step is still a `Node` whose L1 behavior is Bind (std/node.dag). The
// coordination fact is carried by the type of the Bind wrapper:
// `EffectTypedBind<E>`. The `E` parameter is the effect type itself
// (`HttpEffect`, `QueueEffect`, `StreamEffect`, `PubSubEffect`), so the
// effect is part of the value's type signature, not an annotation side
// table and not a behavior variant.
//
// BOUNDS ARE STRUCTURAL. `SettleBound` and `ConvergeBound` use Peano
// `Nat` steps: deterministic simulator ticks / acknowledgement rounds,
// not wall-clock host timing. That keeps async-settlement and eventual-
// consistency checks replayable by a generated simulator. If a producer
// cannot express the bound, the producer returns `Outcome.Rejected` with
// a Diagnostic; it must not fabricate an unbounded/default value.
//
// REFERENCES ARE OPAQUE SYMBOLS. `LanguageRef`, `FrameworkRef`, and
// `NetworkAddress` are local typed carriers over kernel-ambient `Symbol`.
// They are name references into extdeps/languages, extdeps/frameworks, and
// the deployment boundary respectively. This is the same K-1 discipline as
// std/node.dag: coordination consumers compare identity; human-readable
// spellings stay at rendering / external-boundary layers.
//
// OPTIONAL FRAMEWORK WITHOUT `Option`. The frozen scaffold used the
// phrase "optional FrameworkRef". The modeled carrier below is
// `FrameworkBinding = HostedByFramework | NoFramework`. That preserves
// the external fact while avoiding a bare optional slot whose absence
// could be silently ignored. Consumers must match both arms.
//
// FALLIBLE BOUNDARY CONSTRUCTION. This file does not mint a local
// `CoordinationResult`; `CoordinationBoundary<T>` is an alias-identity
// onto `Outcome<T>` from std/diagnostic.dag. Any producer that validates
// endpoint addresses, language/framework references, or wire-contract
// compatibility returns this shared success|Diagnostic carrier.
// Re-declaring success/failure payloads here would duplicate the
// fail-closed carrier authority.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// References and endpoint carrier


type NetworkAddress {
  identity: Symbol
}


// CoordinationBoundary<T> — namespace alias only. The result shape is
// owned by std/diagnostic.dag's `Outcome<T>`.
type CoordinationBoundary<T> = Outcome<T>


type LanguageRef {
  identity: Symbol
}


type FrameworkRef {
  identity: Symbol
}


// FrameworkBinding — the framework coordinate on an endpoint.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1) — 🟢 GREEN
// (terminal). Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every endpoint consumer needs to know
//      whether framework semantics apply.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `Bool` or optional slot would hide the
//      framework identity when present, or allow absence to be skipped.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: framework hosting is not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an endpoint is either framework-hosted or
//      not; the two cases are exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two arms are heterogeneous
//      (one carries a FrameworkRef, one carries no payload).
type FrameworkBinding
  = HostedByFramework { framework: FrameworkRef }
  | NoFramework


type Endpoint {
  address: NetworkAddress,
  language: LanguageRef,
  framework: FrameworkBinding
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Coordination semantics


type SettleBound {
  steps: Nat
}


type ConvergeBound {
  steps: Nat
}


// CoordinationSemantics — the closed messaging/coordination partition.
//
// Anchors: request-reply, fire-and-forget, publish-subscribe, stream/pipe,
// and eventual consistency. The non-immediate variants carry their
// deterministic simulator bound structurally.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1) — 🟢 GREEN
// (terminal). Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the wire contract's coordination mode is
//      shared by simulator, emitter, and verification consumers.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: flattening to flags like
//      `{ sync, async, stream, pubsub, bound? }` admits contradictory
//      modes and missing bounds.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the modes are messaging-pattern
//      alternatives, not projections from a richer algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one wire contract has exactly one
//      coordination semantic at this layer; the modes are alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are not mechanical
//      copies over a declared parameter set; two carry distinct bounds.
type CoordinationSemantics
  = Sync
  | Async { settle: SettleBound }
  | Stream
  | PubSub
  | EventuallyConsistent { converge: ConvergeBound }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Effect typing for Bind composition


type HttpEffect {
  contract: CoordinationSemantics
}


type QueueEffect {
  contract: CoordinationSemantics
}


type StreamEffect {
  contract: CoordinationSemantics
}


type PubSubEffect {
  contract: CoordinationSemantics
}


// CoordinationEffect — the closed effect family accepted by
// `EffectTypedBind<E>` in this file's scope. The individual variants are
// also first-class types so a consumer can require exactly `HttpEffect`
// or exactly `StreamEffect` at a boundary.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1) — 🟢 GREEN
// (terminal). Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: transport/effect family is a shared
//      dispatch fact for Bind consumers.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a string or flag set would allow
//      invalid combinations and would not carry typed payloads.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: these are coordination effect families,
//      not a richer std/algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one coordination effect inhabits exactly
//      one family at this layer.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the payload shapes are not a
//      uniform `Effect<Kind>` projection over a declared closed set.
type CoordinationEffect
  = Http { effect: HttpEffect }
  | Queue { effect: QueueEffect }
  | Streamed { effect: StreamEffect }
  | Published { effect: PubSubEffect }


type EffectTypedBind<E> {
  bind: Node,
  effect: E
}


type HttpBind = EffectTypedBind<HttpEffect>
type QueueBind = EffectTypedBind<QueueEffect>
type StreamBind = EffectTypedBind<StreamEffect>
type PubSubBind = EffectTypedBind<PubSubEffect>


// EndpointEffectClass — idempotency evidence at the endpoint boundary.
// It is partitioned, not a boolean, matching the v3 effects.dag
// idempotent-vs-breaking shape. The evidence can be consumed by the
// idempotency lens without recomputing the transport family from strings.
type EndpointEffectClass<E>
  = EndpointIdempotent { effect: E }
  | EndpointBreaking { effect: E }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Wire contracts and deployment unit


type WireContract {
  from: Endpoint,
  to: Endpoint,
  semantics: CoordinationSemantics
}


type DeploymentUnit {
  endpoints: List<Endpoint>,
  wire_contracts: List<WireContract>
}


// Boundary operation signatures stay comment-prose in the v2-bridge era
// per DECISIONS.md PARSE-1: frozen v2 rejects bodiless `fn`, while v4's
// future parser will admit checked signatures. Consumers that validate a
// deployment or construct a typed Bind must use the shared fail-closed
// carrier:
//
//   validate_deployment : DeploymentUnit -> Outcome<DeploymentUnit>
//   type_http_bind      : (Node, HttpEffect) -> Outcome<HttpBind>
//   type_queue_bind     : (Node, QueueEffect) -> Outcome<QueueBind>
//   type_stream_bind    : (Node, StreamEffect) -> Outcome<StreamBind>
//   type_pubsub_bind    : (Node, PubSubEffect) -> Outcome<PubSubBind>
